# DrawIO MCP Server Project Rules

## Testing
- Always write a test if a functionality requires drawio manipulation (glueing connectors, changing colours, cells going to different pages, etc.).
- After any slightly relevant change, run all tests.
- Create and manage a central helper .sh tester for consistent test execution.
- Test suites should be easy to run with one click:
  - One test suite should assert the core functionality and diagram manipulations
  - One should check the stdio server code
  - Another should check the SSE code

## Code Style
- Use pure functions and functional programming as much as possible.
- DrawIO/XML manipulations should be done by pure functions.
- Write reusable code whenever possible.
- Modularize files so that each file does not exceed 800-1000 lines.
- Discourage creating too many files unless a specific file or logic needs to be separate.

## Documentation
- Maintain a learning folder and update it with lessons learned.
- Update the learning folder whenever we successfully debug or fix something (with user approval).
- Always keep the README up-to-date.
- When needed, separate documentation into manageable components.

## Environment
- Work in a containerized environment (conda for Python).
- Do not edit system files unless explicitly approved.

